fix(hwmon): identify an i2c client by its adapter, not by the bus number

A hwmon device that hangs off an i2c client resolves to a canonical path
carrying the kernel-assigned bus number, `.../i2c-15/15-0050`. That number
follows adapter registration order, so on a machine whose graphics drivers
register DDC and AUX adapters it moves between boots: the SMBus adapter and
every client on it are re-keyed, and the saved settings of the DDR5 SPD
temperature sensors are orphaned. A custom sensor built on them then reports
MISSING_TEMP_FAILSAFE and the fans it drives run at full speed.

Measured on one machine across fifty-six retained boots: the SPD clients
landed on i2c-16 forty-four times and on i2c-15 twelve times, decided by
whether the NVIDIA driver or i2c_piix4 registered first.

This is the instability 5.0.0 fixed for SCSI disks, whose canonical path
embeds a probe-order hostN, and it takes the same shape: a rung above the
location fallback, left out of the legacy chain so migrate_legacy_device_uid
carries existing settings onto the new identity exactly once.

The adapter's own name is in the identifier because one controller registers
several ports (`SMBus PIIX4 adapter port 0 at 0b00`, `port 2 at 0b00`,
`port 1 at 0b20`) under a single PCI function, and the same client address
can appear on more than one, so the parent path and address alone collide.

